  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a cohort from a single region, Taiwan, which may limit the generalizability of the specific developmental windows and mediation percentages to other geographic or climatic contexts.",
      "what": "This longitudinal cohort study analyzed 57,056 observations from 39,780 participants aged 6 to 25 in Taiwan between 2000 and 2022 to evaluate how life-course exposure to greenness, measured via a two-year average NDVI, affects adult respiratory health. The analysis found that each interquartile range increase in NDVI was associated with 17% to 29% lower odds of lung function impairment, alongside increases of 1.88% in FVC, 1.99% in FEV1, and 1.92% in MMEF. Life-course modeling identified adolescence as the critical period where greenness exposure has the strongest association with adult lung function, with PM2.5 reduction mediating 4.83% to 11.84% of the overall benefit.",
      "why": "It establishes a developmental timeline for environmental health benefits, demonstrating that adolescent exposure to green space is particularly critical for adult lung function and that air pollution mitigation is a key pathway for this effect."
    }
  },
